  "grammar": [
    {
      "id": "n5-u06-g01",
      "title": "い-adjectives — \"is big\", \"isn't big\"",
      "pattern": "N は い-adj です / い-adj (drop い) + くないです",
      "meaning": "N is (not) adj",
      "explanation": "Japanese has two kinds of adjectives. い-adjectives always end in い, like 高い (expensive) and おいしい (delicious). To describe something, put the adjective before です: このかばんは高いです. To make it negative, drop the final い and add くないです: 高い → 高くないです. The です just adds politeness — the adjective itself carries the meaning.",
      "notes": [
        "いい (good) is irregular: the negative is よくないです, never いくないです.",
        "高くありません means the same as 高くないです and is a little more formal.",
        "With 多い and 少ない you will often see this shape: 東京は人が多いです — \"Tokyo has many people.\""
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "jp": "この本[ほん]はおもしろいです。",
          "en": "This book is interesting."
        },
